{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01086 - MONDO_5984",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the treatment of Tinea Infection, a fungal skin condition, through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to Tinea Pedis, a specific form of tinea affecting the feet. The evidence suggests that Benzocaine is used in conjunction with other antifungal agents such as Sulconazole, Econazole, and Oxiconazole, all of which are indicated for Tinea Infection and subsequently for Tinea Pedis. This pathway implies that Benzocaine may serve as a supportive or adjunctive therapy in the management of Tinea Pedis, possibly by enhancing the antifungal effects of these agents. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, albeit indirect, relationship between Benzocaine and Tinea Pedis, likely mediated through its role in treating the underlying infection. The mechanism of action appears to involve the use of Benzocaine in the context of antifungal treatment, where it may contribute to the overall therapeutic effect on the fungal infection that manifests as Tinea Pedis.",
